    // SOS extended – scenarios
    sos_scenario_stress_label:"Acute stress",
    sos_scenario_stress_desc:"Your nervous system is in alarm mode. One minute is enough to turn the corner.",
    sos_scenario_stress_bl:"Extended exhale",
    sos_scenario_stress_step1:"Breathe in through your nose for 4 seconds – belly rises",
    sos_scenario_stress_step2:"Breathe out slowly for 8 seconds – as long as possible",
    sos_scenario_stress_step3:"Repeat 3–5 times – your parasympathetic system kicks in",
    sos_scenario_stress_step4:"Key: exhale twice as long as inhale",
    sos_scenario_stress_tip:"The extended exhale directly activates the vagus nerve. That's biology, not magic.",
    sos_scenario_anger_label:"Anger & frustration",
    sos_scenario_anger_desc:"Anger is energy – it needs a channel. Calm down first, then act.",
    sos_scenario_anger_bl:"Physiological sigh",
    sos_scenario_anger_bd:"deep in · follow-up breath · long out",
    sos_scenario_anger_step1:"Briefly separate yourself from the situation – different room or outside",
    sos_scenario_anger_step2:"Breathe in deeply through the nose – then pull in a little more",
    sos_scenario_anger_step3:"Forcefully exhale through the mouth – let it all out",
    sos_scenario_anger_step4:"Consciously relax jaw, shoulders, and hands",
    sos_scenario_anger_tip:"Anger under 90 seconds is a body reflex. After that, it's a choice.",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_label:"Anxiety & panic",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_desc:"Anxiety exaggerates. Your body thinks it's in danger – it isn't.",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_bl:"Box breathing",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_step1:"Name 5 things you see right now – out loud or in your head",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_step2:"Name 4 things you can touch – touch them",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_step3:"Name 3 things you hear right now",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_step4:"Then: 4s in – 4s hold – 4s out – 4s hold",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_tip:"The 5-4-3 technique pulls your brain out of the anxiety loop into the present. Box breathing keeps it there.",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_label:"Overwhelm",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_desc:"Too much at once. Your mind is spinning. Let everything go – for now.",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_bl:"Breath counting",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_step1:"Write or think: what is the ONE most important thing right now?",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_step2:"Everything else doesn't exist for the next minute",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_step3:"Count quietly: In (1) – Out (2) – up to 10, then restart",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_step4:"When thoughts arise: notice, return to counting",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_tip:"Counting gives your mind a task – this stops the carousel more reliably than 'don't think'.",
    sos_scenario_panic_label:"Panic attack",
    sos_scenario_panic_desc:"Panic ends. Always. It feels worse than it is – your body is deceiving you.",
    sos_scenario_panic_bl:"Diaphragmatic breathing",
    sos_scenario_panic_step1:"Place one hand on your chest – feel your heartbeat",
    sos_scenario_panic_step2:"You are safe. This attack will end within minutes",
    sos_scenario_panic_step3:"Breathe calmly and evenly – 4s in, 6s out, no holding",
    sos_scenario_panic_step4:"Say inwardly: My body is protecting me. This will pass.",
    sos_scenario_panic_tip:"Deep breathing or breath-holding can intensify panic. Diaphragmatic breathing is gentle and without holding.",
    sos_before_start:"Before you start",
    sos_before_step1:"Find a quiet spot – sit or stand comfortably",
    sos_before_step2:"Put your phone down or place it in front of you – no scrolling",
    sos_before_step3:"Briefly close your eyes and arrive",
    sos_before_step4:"Take one deep breath – then start",
    sos_start_btn:"🫁 Start {{bl}}",
    sos_disclaimer:"SOS is not a substitute for professional help.\nFor persistent symptoms, consult a doctor.",
