    sos_scenario_stress_label:"Acute stress",
    sos_scenario_stress_desc:"Your nervous system is in alarm mode. One minute is enough to turn the corner.",
    sos_scenario_stress_bl:"Extended exhale",
    sos_scenario_stress_tip:"The extended exhale directly activates the vagus nerve. That's biology, not magic.",
    sos_scenario_anger_label:"Anger & frustration",
    sos_scenario_anger_desc:"Anger is energy – it needs a channel. Calm down first, then act.",
    sos_scenario_anger_bl:"Physiological sigh",
    sos_scenario_anger_bd:"deep in · follow-up breath · long out",
    sos_scenario_anger_tip:"Anger under 90 seconds is a body reflex. After that, it's a choice.",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_label:"Anxiety & panic",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_desc:"Anxiety exaggerates. Your body thinks it's in danger – it isn't.",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_bl:"Box breathing",
    sos_scenario_anxiety_tip:"The 5-4-3 technique pulls your brain out of the anxiety loop into the present. Box breathing keeps it there.",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_label:"Overwhelm",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_desc:"Too much at once. Your mind is spinning. Let everything go – for now.",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_bl:"Breath counting",
    sos_scenario_overwhelm_tip:"Counting gives your mind a task – this stops the carousel more reliably than 'don't think'.",
    sos_scenario_panic_label:"Panic attack",
    sos_scenario_panic_desc:"Panic ends. Always. It feels worse than it is – your body is deceiving you.",
    sos_scenario_panic_bl:"Diaphragmatic breathing",
    sos_scenario_panic_tip:"Deep breathing or breath-holding can intensify panic. Diaphragmatic breathing is gentle and without holding.",
